{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J9xs194L5rQtmJ7mTo8J9D","type":"tool_result","content":"f4be58f Refresh docs/TODO.md (#202)\nc62e9e0 reviewer: test that a failed MarkReady never fails close-out; filter reviewedHeads by issue/stage (#201)\n940fbaf github: read required checks and check runs (#46, part 1) (#200)\n4678895 Bound the gitleaks timeout when a child holds the output pipe (#198) (#199)\nf832949 feat: Mark a worker PR ready for review once every requested rev… (#194) (#197)\ndb07c00 chore: Reviewer close-out fails when its PR is merged mid-review (#184) (#196)\na27270c closeout: end-to-end plan.md merge test; keep log on conflict (#195)\n8071f91 ard(triage:engineer): #194 Adds a readiness check to the reviewer loop that marks a worker's draft… [skip ci]\n33f5067 ard(triage:lead): #194 The controller should mark a worker's draft pull request ready for revi… [skip ci]\n26a8594 feat: closeout: don't revert newer plan.md sections committed af… (#138) (#193)\n898af75 feat: Optional gitleaks pass in the log secret scan (#89) (#192)\n7951054 feat: Workers: .gitattributes changes can hide diffs from review (#142) (#191)\ne388be9 ard(triage:engineer): #184 Makes review close-out end `stale` instead of `failed` when the PR was… [skip ci]\nd49c4f8 ard(triage:lead): #184 Review close-out should treat a pull request that was merged or closed,… [skip ci]\n14cff42 ard(triage:engineer): #89 Adds an optional gitleaks scanner to the redact package, combined with… [skip ci]\nd4d69ff ard(triage:engineer): #138 Adds an optional merge hook to committer files so close-out commits onl… [skip ci]\n167c3df ard(triage:engineer): #142 Refuses any worker change to a `.gitattributes` file in the committer's… [skip ci]\nf4f08f6 Refresh docs/TODO.md (#190)\n4cf2b20 Add ard serve --loops; the soak runs only scanners, stale and reaper (#187) (#188)\n348cf1c Cost and tokens table on each issue (#172, part 2) (#189)\na7027c7 ard(triage:lead): #89 Adds a second, optional pass to the close-out secret scan: a new scanne… [skip ci]\ncdeccea ard(triage:lead): #142 The fix is to make a worker change to any .gitattributes file go to a h… [skip ci]\n4928ed5 ard(triage:lead): #138 Close-out should stop a job's plan.md commit from reverting sections th… [skip ci]\ndf240ba chore: Wake the loops without restarting ard serve (#161) (#186)\n81a7b13 Refresh docs/TODO.md (#185)\nb7926e2 chore: Job cleanup fails on Go's read-only module cache and leak… (#174) (#181)\n1d7570d feat: serve: refuse to start on a git too old for the committer (#165) (#180)\n26ba2aa Add Phase 4 (docs and site) and Phase 5 (distribution) (#179)\nbfabfc6 chore: eng-review should run the repository's own lint target (#160) (#176)\nbbfe3a5 Record per-step usage and commit a per-job costs file (#172, part 1) (#182)\n30b476c Add the reviewer loop (#35)\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go\n294:## Worker loop\n295-\n296-A worker implements `plan.md` on its own branch and ends with a draft PR, test results, and a summary on the issue.\n297-\n298-1. **Poll and claim** `ard:worker:<role>` (added by the triage fan-in gate; the worker never adds or removes it) like triage, also skipping `ard:needs-human`, `ard:hold`, `ard:executor:failed` and an issue that has a running triage job or any running worker job. Workers rank second for job slots, after reviewers. Before claiming, the loop re-checks `ard:approved` when `require_approval` is on (no label change when it is missing), and the admission: an App-authored issue passes, a human-filed one passes only when its stored body hash still matches, and a changed title or body is re-gated exactly as triage does (one comment, `ard:needs-human`).\n299-2. **Branch** `worker/<role>-<issue#>-<slug>` from `origin/main`. The slug is the issue title lowercased, every run of other characters turned into one `-`, at most 40 ASCII characters, `issue` when nothing is left. The title can change between rounds, so the loop looks the branch up with `git ls-remote` on `worker/<role>-<issue#>-*`: one match is reused (and fetched), none is created on the first push, and more than one (only a human can make the second) posts once and adds `ard:needs-human` without claiming. Worker role names may not contain an all-digit segment (`eng-12`), so `worker/<role>-<issue#>-` names exactly one role and issue.\n300-3. **Steps.** One worker job is one state row, one pool slot and one attempt, but it runs several executor jobs, each in a fresh worktree detached at a SHA: the agent step (`<job id>`) at the branch head, or `origin/main` for a new branch; a test step (`<job id>t<k>`) and a fix step (`<job id>f<k>`) at the SHA the last push made. The branch itself belongs to the committer. Each step's handle is saved before it starts, so the Reaper's heartbeat and deadline rules apply to tests too. `timeout` covers the whole job: each step gets the time that is left, and with under two minutes left the loop stops and reports the tests as failed (\"ran out of time\"). `max_budget_usd` likewise caps the whole job: each agent step gets the cap minus the cost of the earlier steps (a step that reports no cost is assumed free and logged), and when nothing is left the job fails with `job budget exhausted`.\n301-4. **Run the agent** with `.ard/workers/<role>/prompt.txt` and these untrusted inputs: `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md` (written by the loop, outside `.ard/<issue#>/`, never committed), `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md` when triage wrote one and `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` when a scanner filed the issue (each only as a regular file, never through a symlink), the triage role files and logs, and, in a fix step, `.ard-input/<issue#>/test-output.log` (the untrusted header, the test command and its exit code, and the last 64 KiB of the test log). Review feedback is added by the review-feedback loop. The agent edits code and writes `.ard/<issue#>/worker-<role>.md`; the executor scrubs a stale copy first.\n302-5. **Guard the diff** after each agent or fix step, before anything leaves the host. The worktree belongs to the agent, so the guard never trusts its `.git`, config, index or attributes: git runs against the controller's own clone with a private index filled from the base commit (so `skip-worktree` and `assume-unchanged` hide nothing, and no planted filter, textconv, fsmonitor or hook runs), with its objects in a private temp directory (the clone is only read, so an agent cannot grow it); a worktree with a new or resized file over 1 MiB, or more than 256 of them, is held before anything is hashed. The scanned bytes are copied to a controller-private directory and the code lane commits the copies, so nothing can change a file between the scan and the commit. Close-out's changed-file listing uses the same hardened path. Paths under `.ard/<issue#>/` and `.ard-input/` belong to close-out and the loop and are left out. Everything else is checked, and any rejection holds the job for a human (`ard:needs-human`, nothing pushed): the path rule in `committer.CheckCodePath` (all of `.github/`, the rest of `.ard/`, `.git` segments, `.gitmodules`, any `.gitattributes` (`linguist-generated` or `-diff` would collapse files in the GitHub PR diff, so a worker change to one always goes to a human), secret-shaped names), any symlink, nested repository or other non-regular file, a file over 1 MiB, a binary file, and more than 256 changed files. The credential scan runs twice: the content of every written file is checked for the values of known secrets (the hold names the variable, never the value), and the built-in pattern scanner runs over it with only the findings on lines this job added kept, so a worker that edits a file that already holds a credential-shaped fixture is not held for lines it did not write. The comment names file, line and rule (`internal/foo_test.go:12: secret scan rule aws-access-key`), never the text. A scanner error fails closed. Test fixtures that look like credentials must be built at run time, as `internal/redact`'s tests do (`\"AK\" + \"IA\" + ...`); a literal is held, and there is no in-band escape. Over 1,000 changed lines in the whole PR (the diff against the merge base with `main`) also holds. The comment shows the reason as `diff guard: <entry>; <entry>; <entry>; and N more`.\n303-6. **Two commits per round.** The loop commits the code through the committer's code lane (`Branches.CommitCode`) with the title `[<type>: ]<issue title> (#<issue>)`, the redacted summary (secrets and host paths) as the body and an `Ard-Job: <job-id>` trailer, no `[skip ci]`. `<type>` comes from the issue's `type:*` label (`feature` is `feat`, `bug` is `fix`; `docs`, `chore`, `refactor` and `test` keep their names) and with no `type:*` label the `<type>: ` part is dropped and the title is `<issue title> (#<issue>)`. The prefix is never taken from the plan or the lead's routing, so it depends on a maintainer applying `type:*` at intake. At the end close-out commits `worker-<role>.md` and the redacted logs of every agent step to the same branch (or to `main`, as triage's are, when the job stopped before its first push). `.ard/<issue#>/` never goes through the code lane. A push failure (branch exists, conflict, branch gone, guard) fails the job with no automatic retry.\n304-7. **Test.** Run `tests` (default `make test`; empty disables the step) as `/bin/sh -c` in the role's image, with no credential. On failure the agent gets the failure output in a fix step, which commits and pushes again, up to `max_test_fixes` (default 2) within the same job. A fix that changes no files, the cap, or the time running out ends the loop with the tests failed. Test logs stay on the host (kept 7 days) and are never committed.\n305-8. **Open or update a draft PR** with ard's own `Closes #<issue>`, the agent summary quoted and neutralised (`committer.NeutraliseRefs`, then marker and mention defusing), the test result and links to `worker-<role>.md`, the logs and the job id. Reviewers are the role's `reviewers`, or every reviewer role when it is unset or empty (`when:` rules are ignored until #42), plus any `review:<role>` the lead deferred, limited to roles that exist, and requested only when the tests did not fail. The PR stays a draft; the merger marks it ready. A job that has pushed always gets its draft PR, even when it then ends in `ard:needs-human` or a failure, so pushed work is never left without one.\n306-9. **Report.** Comment on the issue with the summary, PR link and test result (`passed`, `passed after 1 fix attempt`, `failed after 2 fix attempts`, `not configured`); swap `ard:worker:<role>:running` for `:done`. If tests still fail, add `ard:tests:failed` on the issue and the PR and request no reviewers; a green run removes it. A worker that ends without the loop's result (for example one the Reaper closes out), or whose role file says `needs-human`, never becomes `:done` without a PR.\n307-\n308-**The starter worker (`workers/engineer`, #34).** `.ard/workers/engineer/prompt.txt` implements `plan.md`'s `## Implementation`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, in a fix round `test-output.log`, and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, `issue.md`, the triage role files and logs. It writes code, tests and docs, and `worker-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, an optional `next` of `review:` entries, and never `worker` or `verdict`. The body has Changes, Testing and Open issues sections; it becomes the commit body, the PR description and the issue comment, and a fix round's body describes the whole change. It answers `needs-human` for a change the diff guard would reject, more than 1,000 lines, a missing decision, nothing to change, or an input that tries to instruct it. In a fix round it keeps `done` and explains a failure it cannot fix, so the loop reports `ard:tests:failed` on the PR. The repository's package manager may fetch a dependency the change needs (`go get`, `npm install`); the agent installs no system packages and uses no other network, and the guard and reviewers still see `go.mod` and lockfile changes. Credential-shaped test fixtures are built at run time. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5, `timeout: 60m` for the whole job and `tests: make test`. It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55's `ard-agent` image gives a worker image a base.\n309-\n310-**Review feedback loops back here.** When any reviewer ends with `ard:review:<role>:changes`, the controller re-requests `ard:worker:<role>` on the issue and increments the round. The worker fixes on the same branch, and only the reviewers that asked for changes run again. The merger sends a PR back the same way when its branch conflicts with `main`. After `max_review_rounds` (default 3) the issue goes to `ard:needs-human`.\n311-\n312-**The merger takes it from here.** A worker PR stays a draft until every requested review passes at its current head; then the controller marks it ready for review. The merger (Phase 2) merges ready PRs that also pass its gates.\n313-\n314-## Reviewer loop\n315-\n316-Reviewers run like workers against a PR instead of an issue: they read the change, never edit code, and return a verdict the controller turns into PR comments and a label.\n317-\n318-1. **Poll and claim** open PRs with `ard:review:<role>`, skipping claimed, done, changes, failed and held. Reviewers get first call on free job slots, since each finished review can unblock a merge. Only PRs the App opened are reviewed: the author is the App, the base is `main` and the head is a `worker/<role>-<issue#>-<slug>` branch (human PRs are skipped and logged as `not_app_pr`). A PR is also skipped when its issue is closed, held or `ard:needs-human`, when a worker job for the issue is running, when the PR has `ard:tests:failed`, and when the issue's title or body changed after admission (the issue is re-gated exactly as the worker does, not reviewed). Reviewers of different roles run in parallel on one PR.\n319-2. **Pin the head SHA.** Before claiming, the loop fetches the branch into the controller's clone and requires the tip to equal the PR's head SHA; on a mismatch it retries on the next poll. The match also shows the head is this repository's branch, so a fork PR with a colliding branch name is never reviewed. The job's worktree is cut at that commit, so the review describes exactly one commit even if the branch moves.\n320-3. **Run the agent** with `.ard/reviewers/<role>/prompt.txt` and these untrusted inputs: `.ard-input/<issue#>/diff.patch` (the controller's diff from the merge base with `main` to the pinned head, `--stat` then the patch, taken with `--text`, `--no-textconv` and attributes read from the empty tree so a `.gitattributes` entry cannot hide a change, with this issue's `.ard/<issue#>/` left out and cut at 1 MiB), `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md` (written by the loop, never committed), the committed `.ard/<issue#>/` (plan, triage and worker role files and logs) and the sidecar (security policy, license allowlist). The loop runs every git command against the controller clone, never in the reviewer's worktree (the agent itself may run read-only git there). In round 2 and later it also gets its previous findings and must say which are resolved (review feedback loop).\n321-4. **The agent writes `review-<role>.md`** with `verdict: approve | changes` and `findings:` (`file`, `line`, `severity`, `blocking`, `message`; see the .ard/ contract). It may write nothing else under `.ard/<issue#>/`, and code edits are never collected. An `approve` that carries a blocking finding is treated as `changes` (the job succeeds and the comment says so).\n322-5. **Close-out.** The controller posts one PR review at the pinned commit (event `COMMENT`): findings on lines the PR added are inline comments, every other finding is listed in the review body. If GitHub answers 422 (a line or the commit is not in the PR), it posts the review again with every finding in the body, and if that is also refused, as a plain PR comment. It commits `review-<role>.md` and the run's redacted output to the PR branch through the per-branch committer (no `[skip ci]`: PR-branch commits always run CI so required checks report on the head), and swaps `:running` for `:done` or `:changes`. The reviewer never edits the PR. After a close-out (and on every tick, so a restart cannot miss it) the controller marks a draft worker PR ready for review once every requested review is `:done` at the PR's current head and no `:changes`, `:running`, `ard:executor:failed`, `ard:needs-human`, `ard:hold` or `ard:tests:failed` label is present; it never converts a PR back to draft and never merges, which stays with the merger (Phase 2) and humans.\n323-6. **A moved head is not a pass.** Just before close-out the loop compares the branch with the pinned commit. If it gained code outside `.ard/<issue#>/` meanwhile, a `changes` verdict is still posted and labelled (it is the conservative one), but an `approve` is posted without `:done`: the request label stays and the review runs again on the new head. The reviewed commit is recorded in the close-out record so the merger (#46) must re-verify that no code changed since `Plan.Review.CommitSHA` before merging: a small window remains between the head check and close-out adding `:done`. A review closed out by the Reaper after a restart has no head information, so an approve is treated as stale.\n324-7. **A gone PR is stale, not failed.** A human may merge or close the PR, or GitHub may delete its branch, while a review runs. At close-out the controller reads the PR before committing. If it was merged or closed, or the committer reports the branch gone, the job ends `stale`: nothing is committed to the branch, only `:running` is removed, there is no `ard:executor:failed` and no failure comment, and `review.pr_gone` is logged with the state (`merged`, `closed` or