en:`, so every PR from the starter worker gets it. It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55.\n327-\n328-**The label is the gate, not GitHub's \"request changes\".** A bot's request-changes review blocks merge until someone dismisses it, even after the fix. Reviews post as comments; `ard:review:<role>:changes` is what sends the PR back to the worker.\n329-\n330-**Pair each reviewer with a deterministic tool where one exists.** The tool finds; the agent explains, ranks and checks the result against the plan.\n331-\n332-| Reviewer | Runs when | Deterministic aid | Blocks on |\n333-| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n334-| security-review | always | semgrep or gosec, secret scan | injection, authz gaps, secrets, unsafe dependencies |\n--\n339:Every directory under `.ard/reviewers/` reviews every PR unless `config.yaml` gives it a `when:` rule (paths, or a triage role that ran). The lead can request extra reviewers through `next`; they are recorded in the triage job's close-out record in local state (`closeout.Deferred` reads them) and the worker loop adds the `ard:review:<role>` labels to the PR when it opens it.\n340-\n341-## Merger loop\n342-\n343-The merger is controller code, not an agent: every 5 minutes it merges each PR that has cleared every gate, and the merge closes the issue.\n344-\n345-A PR merges when all of these hold:\n346-\n347-- every requested `ard:review:<role>` is `:done` and none is `:changes`\n348-- required status checks are green on the current head\n349-- the branch is up to date with `main`; if it is behind, the merger updates it and waits for checks on the new head\n350-- it carries none of `ard:hold`, `ard:needs-human`, `ard:tests:failed` or `ard:executor:failed`\n351-- the diff touches no protected path; the list comes only from `merge.protected_paths`, with no floor in code (decided, #3), so a human who empties the list removes the gate. `ard init` proposes the defaults `.github/`, `.ard/config.yaml`, the four role trees (`.ard/scanners/`, `.ard/triage/`, `.ard/workers/`, `.ard/reviewers/`), `**/CODEOWNERS`, and the agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents: `CLAUDE.md`, `**/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `**/AGENTS.md`, `.claude/` and `.mcp.json` (13 entries; `**/X` already matches a root `X`, the bare entries are kept for clarity). An entry ending in `/` is a directory prefix; any other entry is a doublestar glob. Matching is case-sensitive on the repo-relative path. A prefix matches on a directory boundary (`.github/` matches `.github` and `.github/x`, never `.githubx`). A glob follows doublestar: `*` stays within one path segment and `**` crosses them, so a bare `CODEOWNERS` would match only the root file. Quote globs that start with `*` in YAML. The merger checks both names of a renamed file; a malformed path counts as protected. A PR that touches one gets `ard:needs-approval` and merges on the next poll after a human approves\n352-\n353-It squash-merges with the worker's commit title and the `Ard-Job` trailer, then deletes the branch, so `.ard/<issue#>/` and its logs land on `main` with the code. If updating the branch conflicts, it re-requests the worker for a rebase round, which counts toward `max_review_rounds`.\n354-\n355-**Branch protection and the bot.** The committer pushes `.ard/<issue#>/` commits straight to `main` with `[skip ci]`, which neither a \"require a pull request\" rule nor a required status check can admit, so the App must be on the bypass list of `main`'s ruleset (mode Always). GitHub's bypass covers the whole ruleset, not single rules (found on Gate 2, #39). ard keeps that bypass narrow itself: the committer refuses any path outside `.ard/<issue#>/`, the merger merges only when every review passed and required checks are green on the current head (checked in code, not left to GitHub), and the bot-commit audit flags any bot commit on `main` that touches anything else.\n356-","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session