en rounds, so the loop looks the branch up with `git ls-remote` on `worker/<role>-<issue#>-*`: one match is reused (and fetched), none is created on the first push, and more than one (only a human can make the second) posts once and adds `ard:needs-human` without claiming. Worker role names may not contain an all-digit segment (`eng-12`), so `worker/<role>-<issue#>-` names exactly one role and issue.\n300-3. **Steps.** One worker job is one state row, one pool slot and one attempt, but it runs several executor jobs, each in a fresh worktree detached at a SHA: the agent step (`<job id>`) at the branch head, or `origin/main` for a new branch; a test step (`<job id>t<k>`) and a fix step (`<job id>f<k>`) at the SHA the last push made. The branch itself belongs to the committer. Each step's handle is saved before it starts, so the Reaper's heartbeat and deadline rules apply to tests too. `timeout` covers the whole job: each step gets the time that is left, and with under two minutes left the loop stops and reports the tests as failed (\"ran out of time\"). `max_budget_usd` likewise caps the whole job: each agent step gets the cap minus the cost of the earlier steps (a step that reports no cost is assumed free and logged), and when nothing is left the job fails with `job budget exhausted`.\n301-4. **Run the agent** with `.ard/workers/<role>/prompt.txt` and these untrusted inputs: `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md` (written by the loop, outside `.ard/<issue#>/`, never committed), `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md` when triage wrote one and `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` when a scanner filed the issue (each only as a regular file, never through a symlink), the triage role files and logs, and, in a fix step, `.ard-input/<issue#>/test-output.log` (the untrusted header, the test command and its exit code, and the last 64 KiB of the test log). Review feedback is added by the review-feedback loop. The agent edits code and writes `.ard/<issue#>/worker-<role>.md`; the executor scrubs